WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.325
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.951 --> 00:00:09.450
ETHAN SOMMER: Hi,
I am Ethan Sommer.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.420
I'm a director of engineering
at Target for external cloud

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.730
and containers as a service.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:19.850
And I'm going to tell you today
a little bit about Target's

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.140
journey with Kubernetes.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.750
And Jay is going
to tell the story

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:29.710
about how it's gone as we've
deployed that into our stores.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.900
So I'm going to
talk specifically

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:37.090
about how Target is using
Kubernetes in our data centers,

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:41.950
in the public cloud, and in
our stores across the country.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.720
I'm going to talk about
how Target decided

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.980
to bet on Kubernetes and kind
of what our journey has been

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.600
in our data centers in Google
and at Google Compute Engine

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.570
and in our stores.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.320
And I'm going to tell you kind
of what sorts of challenges

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.040
we ran into on those
different places

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.290
and what we learned as we
went through that, because I

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.960
think it's a similar story to
what most organizations would

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:09.459
see.

00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:12.220
But this is how
we've experienced it.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:18.140
So how did we get to using
Kubernetes in the first place?

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.110
About-- I don't know--
four or five years ago,

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:28.280
Target started to kind of
try to adopt DevOps and agile

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.910
practices.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:33.920
And part of that was
trying to move away

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:39.260
from kind of a traditional
dev and ops model

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:42.530
and to try to empower the
development teams to do

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.280
more of the ops work.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.150
And part of that was by
adopting config as code.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:50.990
And to do that, Target
used a lot of Chef.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.405
And Chef was great except
for all of these problems

00:01:53.405 --> 00:01:54.530
that we had with it, right?

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.817
I mean, people used
community cookbooks

00:01:56.817 --> 00:01:59.150
that they didn't understand,
that when they didn't work,

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.710
they didn't know
how to fix them.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.180
They took a long
time to converge.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:08.750
When we were using them
in the public cloud,

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.440
if a autoscaling
group like a node

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:15.564
failed and it got replaced, well
maybe one of those dependencies

00:02:15.564 --> 00:02:16.730
had changed in the meantime.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:18.830
And then it didn't work.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:23.360
And people spent a huge amount
of time trying to make it work.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.617
I think I heard some team--

00:02:24.617 --> 00:02:26.450
you know, maybe they
were being hyperbolic--

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:27.866
but they estimated
that they spent

00:02:27.866 --> 00:02:30.530
80% of their time doing
deployments and 20%

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.645
of their time actually
delivering software.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:39.050
So about a little
over two years ago

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:44.060
we started adopting Spinnaker
and immutable deployments.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.060
And we provided-- basically the
way that immutable deployments

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:48.800
work is that you provide--

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.660
we provided the
teams a base image,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.200
which had met our
security requirements

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.510
and had some agents to do
logging and stuff like that.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.222
And developers
created an RPM file.

00:03:00.222 --> 00:03:02.180
And they knew exactly
what they were including.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.346
They weren't like including
some community cookbook.

00:03:04.346 --> 00:03:10.310
They'd install an RPM
from our operating system.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:12.890
They'd install their
software, then put a config

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:16.970
file onto it-- maybe a
templated config file.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:21.050
And the dependencies are
all resolved at build time.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.760
And builds took like
five to 10 minutes.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:24.260
But then when you
went to go spin up

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:29.460
a new instance of your
program, it took only a couple

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.100
of minutes.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.590
But it was limited
to public clouds.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:35.924
And it took the developer time--

00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:37.381
the time it took
them to figure out

00:03:37.381 --> 00:03:39.440
how to get their stuff
deployed-- from being

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.270
about a month to about a week.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.520
And one of the biggest
problems that we had with it

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.580
was that you can't go
out and buy an O'Reilly

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.111
book about the way that we
combined all of our tools

00:03:49.111 --> 00:03:49.610
together.

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:51.014
We used a lot of
HashiCorp tools.

00:03:51.014 --> 00:03:51.680
And we still do.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.840
This is how we do
VM deployments.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.760
We use a lot of HashiCorp tools.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.040
We use Spinnaker.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.230
We use an ELK stack.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.540
And so the way that we
combined those together

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:04.622
was unique to Target.

00:04:04.622 --> 00:04:06.830
And see, we had to write
all of our own documentation

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:10.100
and teach hundreds of
thousands of developers

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.590
how to use this stack.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.730
Oh, and it was limited
to public clouds

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.990
until Target added OpenStack
support to Spinnaker along

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:21.279
with Veritas.

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:25.540
We added support to Spinnaker,
which is now anyone can use.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:27.970
So now you could use
it in the public cloud

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:31.500
and in the private cloud.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.520
So Kubernetes-- why did
we start using Kubernetes?

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.570
Well, we only provided
the Spinnaker experience

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.989
to our non-commerce digital
clouds using public--

00:04:45.989 --> 00:04:47.405
digital teams using
public clouds.

00:04:47.405 --> 00:04:50.070
So only a fairly
small percentage

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.451
of Target's developers
were able to use Spinnaker

00:04:52.451 --> 00:04:53.700
in this immutable deployments.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.590
And so I actually
got up, because I was

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.550
leading that team at the time.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.960
And I gave a presentation
to everyone and anyone

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.980
who wanted to go in our
technology organization.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:04.980
Hundreds of people came.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:06.996
And I explained
what we were doing.

00:05:06.996 --> 00:05:08.370
And they said,
when can I use it?

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.530
And I said, I don't know.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.410
And so we started
working on making it

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.690
so that we could do the--

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.910
deploy into our
OpenStack environment

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.660
using Spinnaker to
give it to them.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.780
But the other thing
that we did was we

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:25.260
started standing up
Kubernetes in our data center.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:32.580
And that turned out to be
possibly an even bigger kind

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.010
of change in how
people got stuff

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:38.991
out there than our
OpenStack Spinnaker work.

00:05:38.991 --> 00:05:42.730
So in the data
centers developers

00:05:42.730 --> 00:05:45.460
found the experience of using
Kubernetes just irresistible.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.890
It was so much better than
what they were doing with Chef,

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.316
at least based on
how they'd been

00:05:49.316 --> 00:05:50.440
trained on how to use Chef.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:51.648
I'm not trying to knock Chef.

00:05:51.648 --> 00:05:55.810
But the people just found that
they were able to-- instead

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:58.450
of spending 80% of their
time as some people said

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:00.392
working on
deployments, it became

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:02.350
something they spent a
couple hours setting up.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:04.330
And then they basically didn't
think about it after that.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:05.996
They just checked
their stuff in to get.

00:06:05.996 --> 00:06:08.700
And it wound up in Kubernetes.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.990
And they could move on
and start paying attention

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:13.810
to making their
application work.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:16.600
And even as we were struggling--
the team that manages

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:17.950
Kubernetes at Target--

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:20.080
with some performance
problems, which

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:21.869
I'll talk about in
a little bit, they

00:06:21.869 --> 00:06:23.410
were still willing
to put up with it,

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.620
because it made their
job that much better.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:29.350
Application teams were
much more likely to build

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:32.500
resilient applications
too because of the kind

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:35.380
of constructs that
Kubernetes has.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:40.180
Application teams were steered
towards building things

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:43.090
where we told them,
hey, you know that each

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:45.220
pod you put out there?

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:48.107
Just assume that it might
get killed at anytime, right?

00:06:48.107 --> 00:06:49.690
Make sure all your
stuff is stateless.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:54.382
And as a result in comparison
to our OpenStack environment,

00:06:54.382 --> 00:06:55.840
where I think that
people generally

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.680
thought of things as living
there until they didn't

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.290
unless something went
wrong, we told them, just

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:03.910
assume it's going to die.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:07.540
And as a result they
built better applications.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:10.270
And eventually we worked
out the performance problems

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:11.500
more or less.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.500
And everyone lived
happily ever after.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.690
But I've got a few more details.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:20.630
So here's an actual
quote from a developer.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:23.590
He said that "Kubernetes
terrifies me, to be honest.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:25.130
Getting things
running on Minikube

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.130
took so little time and
energy that I don't feel

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:28.690
like I learned much about it.

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:31.330
Am I crazy to have this
fear that it works?

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:33.040
I have no idea why.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.182
What happens when
it doesn't work?"

00:07:35.182 --> 00:07:36.640
And so that just
kind of shows that

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:38.290
like when people
worked with Chef,

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:39.560
it took them a long time to--

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:43.300
or before that, it took them a
long time to get stuff working.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.820
And so they felt like they kind
of understood how it worked.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:47.830
And with Kubernetes they
followed our directions.

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.163
Or they bought an O'Reilly book.

00:07:49.163 --> 00:07:50.861
And they've follow
those directions.

00:07:50.861 --> 00:07:53.110
And when they followed the
directions, it just worked.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:58.930
So Kubernetes in
our data centers--

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:02.130
so what challenges did we have?

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.010
We had problems with
ingress scaling.

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:05.870
I mean one of the
things that we ran into

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:09.770
was that as we were growing--

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:11.840
like everything
worked great before we

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:13.700
had any applications using it.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:15.920
And then we would start
running into problems when we

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.470
had 20 applications using it.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:20.570
And then we'd go and
fix those problems.

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:23.750
And then we'd have problems when
we had 50 applications or 200

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:25.310
applications.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.590
And you know a lot of those
things come from things

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:30.240
like there were
performance problems.

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.638
Some of them-- we've been
doing Kubernetes for a while.

00:08:32.638 --> 00:08:35.179
Some of them have been fixed in
newer versions of Kubernetes.

00:08:35.179 --> 00:08:37.470
Some of them are just kind
of inherent to the idea that

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:40.490
like if you use the
NGINX Ingress Controller

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:44.300
if you have thousands of pods
that are dynamically changing

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:48.110
every time it does that, there's
a 20,000 line config file

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:49.910
that NGINX needs to reload.

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.320
And NGINX doesn't
handle that perfectly.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.539
And so we've been
going through and kind

00:08:54.539 --> 00:08:56.330
of addressing these
issues as they came up.

00:09:00.018 --> 00:09:01.470
That's enough for that.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:04.250
So here are some stats about
Target's use of Kubernetes

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:05.470
in our data centers.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:09.440
We have-- as of when I pulled
this a week or two ago--

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.340
in our test clusters
in the data centers,

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:14.310
we have about 350
different application teams

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:15.960
deploying into Kubernetes.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:18.309
We've got about
4,000 pods running.

00:09:18.309 --> 00:09:20.100
And it's not doing a
whole lot of traffic--

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:23.040
about 260 requests per second.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:26.070
In the production
clusters, there

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:27.450
aren't quite as
many applications

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.190
as some of the
application teams haven't

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.290
gotten to production yet--

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:32.610
about the same number of pods.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.040
And the reason for that
is that things are kind of

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.930
at a larger scale in prod.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.749
But maybe in test, teams
have multiple different

00:09:39.749 --> 00:09:40.290
environments.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:42.590
They might have
dev and stage or--

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.840
and a perf test
environment and whatever.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:47.480
And the number of
requests per second

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:50.950
is higher, not as high as in
some of our other environments

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:51.450
though.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.450
So we run Kubernetes on GCP.

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:59.229
In fact, one of our--

00:09:59.229 --> 00:10:01.270
someone on our account
team told me the other day

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:07.270
that Target is actually the
largest user of Kubernetes

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:10.720
on GCP who is not using GKE.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:14.460
And I'll explain a little
bit why we're not using GKE.

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:16.380
But given that we're
here, it probably

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.890
doesn't surprise you that we're
running Kubernetes on Google

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:19.390
at all.

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:25.940
So some stats about Kubernetes
running on Google Compute--

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:30.530
we've only got 44 applications
there and 35 in prod.

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:32.090
And we've got about 1,000 pods.

00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:35.340
But we handle a lot
more volume there.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:38.360
And we use autoscaling
pretty well within that.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.430
We've got about--
much of Target.com

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:44.820
was running on Kubernetes last
year through our peak season.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.150
And we had a peak of
about 30,000 requests

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:53.560
per second going into
those Kubernetes clusters.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:55.160
And we're more
like 8,000 requests

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:58.290
per second on a typical day
now with kind of organic growth

00:10:58.290 --> 00:10:59.290
and whatever since then.

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:05.030
So what sorts of problems
did we run into in GCP?

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:08.490
So there were some interesting
things that we ran into.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:11.190
We still have the same
sorts of ingress problems

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:14.250
when we're talking from
VMs into Kubernetes.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:16.740
When we're talking from our
external load balancers--

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:22.230
so like a customer with a
web browser talking to us--

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:24.690
then we can use the
Kubernetes integration

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:26.004
with the Google load balancers.

00:11:26.004 --> 00:11:27.420
And all of those
problems go away.

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:33.320
But on the inside, we are
using ingress controllers.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:35.070
And we ran into the
same sorts of problems

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:38.310
we had in the data center.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:41.640
And similarly, we can do a
great job of failing over

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.720
between regions on the outside.

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:46.387
On the inside, some
of the Google tools,

00:11:46.387 --> 00:11:47.970
at least when we
started, were lacking

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.330
to make that easy to do.

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:51.920
So why not use GKE?

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:57.000
So to be clear, I wrote this
slide before this conference.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:02.180
And so back in 2017
when we started,

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:05.450
the role-based access
control within GKE

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:07.210
didn't have the
features that we needed

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:10.534
it to have to meet Target's
security requirements.

00:12:10.534 --> 00:12:11.950
And we told our
account team that.

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:14.510
And they're like, oh, we really
want to make this better.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:16.718
But we went ahead, because
we'd already been doing it

00:12:16.718 --> 00:12:18.340
in the data center.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:20.090
And that's kind of my
second point, right?

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:22.620
We have to have an
on-prem solution.

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:26.570
So we have to make a
distribution of Kubernetes that

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:29.120
works, that integrates well
with Target's systems, that

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:30.574
does all of these things.

00:12:30.574 --> 00:12:32.240
And we have to do it
in the data center.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:34.040
And we use these
immutable deployments,

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:35.270
where we make an RPM.

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:36.570
And then we bake an image.

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:38.570
And then we put-- we do
that in the data center.

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:39.611
And we do that in Google.

00:12:39.611 --> 00:12:42.210
So it was pretty easy for
us to do it in Google.

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:45.950
And so we just went ahead
and deployed the same stuff

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:48.062
that we were doing in the
data center into Google.

00:12:48.062 --> 00:12:50.270
And that worked pretty well
for us particularly given

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:51.710
that Google as I said--

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:53.680
that Google actually
open-sources

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:55.460
all of their integrations
that they have

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:57.330
with Google Cloud components.

00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:00.850
And so a lot of the things
when Google comes up and like

00:13:00.850 --> 00:13:03.380
at Google Next last
year and they said,

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:06.500
GKE can like integrate
with this thing at Google.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:09.680
Well, we got that too, even
though we were using GKE.

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:12.680
So I said I made this slide
before this conference.

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:19.490
Our relationship with Google has
been great when we tell them--

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:21.500
they keep saying, hey,
why don't you use GKE?

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:22.490
And we say, well,
but it's because

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:23.780
of this and this and this.

00:13:23.780 --> 00:13:25.880
And one of those things--
probably the biggest one

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:27.080
at this point--

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:31.800
is, well, we need to run
something in our data center.

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:32.730
So we have to do that.

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:36.610
Well, obviously with GKE
On-Prem when I get back I'm

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:39.030
going to need to
go re-evaluate what

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:42.320
to do in our kind of
medium-term future,

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:44.992
whether that's something
that we want to adopt.

00:13:44.992 --> 00:13:46.450
Now I'm going to
hand it off to Jay

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:51.700
to talk about our experience of
putting Kubernetes in stores.

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:53.350
JAY CHANDRASHEKARAN:
Hello, everyone.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:54.910
I am Jay Chandrashekaran.

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:57.850
I work as a senior
director of technology

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:01.856
at Target managing
the compute services.

00:14:01.856 --> 00:14:07.180
What I am here to talk about
is how we have implemented

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:09.510
compute on the edges.

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:14.650
Ethan talked about
how we use Kubernetes

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:19.150
as a compute platform of
choice both in our data centers

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:22.330
and in GCP.

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:26.500
What I plan to talk here about
is compute on the edges and

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.110
how we use Kubernetes.

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:31.420
Just a little context--

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:33.640
I just want to
understand the audience.

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:38.170
How many of you kind
of work for retailers?

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:38.840
Good.

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:43.420
So and I'm assuming physical
retailers too [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:50.350
How many of you kind of trying
to solve edge compute problems?

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:52.330
Great.

00:14:52.330 --> 00:14:54.910
Probably some of you
have solved it too.

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:57.130
But I just wanted to
understand the context.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:14:59.880
So how I will go
about doing this is--

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:03.210
so I'll-- just for the folks
who are probably not from

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:09.610
the retail background, just a
quick level set in terms of why

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:12.349
we need edge compute, right?

00:15:12.349 --> 00:15:13.390
So that's one part of it.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:15.220
And then I'll give
a quick background

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:17.670
in terms of the
history of how we

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:20.390
did edge compute in the stores.

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:22.900
And then kind of we'll
get into the bit of detail

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:26.130
about how did we
actually implement

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:33.980
the Kubernetes across our 1,800
plus stores, 1,829 to be exact.

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:37.060
So why do we need
edge compute, right?

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:42.070
So we as Target operate
1,800 physical stores.

00:15:42.070 --> 00:15:46.250
Just like an e-commerce
site need to run 24/7,

00:15:46.250 --> 00:15:49.450
we need our stores to be up
and running all the time.

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:52.000
The store systems need to be
up and running all the time so

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:59.590
that guests can actually come in
and buy stuff from the stores.

00:15:59.590 --> 00:16:00.870
If you think about it--

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:03.915
either it is e-commerce
or physical stores--

00:16:03.915 --> 00:16:07.840
a guest who actually comes in
basically does the same thing.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:09.520
They browse items.

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:12.820
They add items to the cart.

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:13.570
They go in.

00:16:13.570 --> 00:16:15.790
And they do checkout.

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:18.610
And what that means for
a physical store is we

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:19.750
need to stock shelves.

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:23.800
So we use a handheld
device called My Device

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:29.307
to enable our team members to
actually stock shelves, right?

00:16:29.307 --> 00:16:30.640
So these are productivity tools.

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:33.280
What I am trying to get to is
a bunch of productivity tools

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:35.560
that our team members use.

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:38.260
And many other retailers
probably know this.

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:39.580
Stores are distributed.

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:41.980
They are spread across
geography, right?

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:44.620
And network outages happen.

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:48.880
But you can't close the store
if a network outage happens.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:53.200
So they need to
operate autonomous way

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:55.400
when such things happen.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:17:01.030
And what that means is we
need to have the ability

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:03.850
to work in a disconnected
fashion, right?

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:07.060
So availability is a big thing.

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:10.569
Second part of it is
response time, right?

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:14.510
So we pride ourselves in
our checkout experience.

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.849
And if you take the other
side of things where our team

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:20.260
members work using
productivity tools,

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:22.420
we want really quick
response time for them

00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:27.050
so that they are not frustrated
with the systems they use.

00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:29.290
And if you take the
checkout example,

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:32.470
we use a metric
called scan to screen.

00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:36.340
It basically says if a
cashier scans an item,

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:39.010
it should actually show up on
the cash register screen in 250

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:40.410
milliseconds, let's say.

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:43.040
And that's sounds great.

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:45.470
But if you think about
once you scan a barcode

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:48.700
what all things need to
happen before it actually

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:49.990
shows up on the screen--

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:51.670
it need to fetch
item information.

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:53.650
It need to calculate the price.

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:55.740
It needs to incorporate
the promotions.

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:58.350
It need to calculate tax before
it actually shows up there.

00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:00.670
It all need to happen and
within 250 milliseconds.

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:06.490
So we probably have a
different kind of issue

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:09.020
to solve from a response
standpoint, right?

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:11.650
So those are two
reasons why we probably

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:14.440
need compute on the
edge, because we just

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:17.170
can't afford to take
the round trip back

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:19.725
to Cloud or to our data center.

00:18:19.725 --> 00:18:23.440
So a bit of history--

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:27.310
how we operated
compute in the stores.

00:18:27.310 --> 00:18:33.490
If you look at five years and
back, we bundle things, right?

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.210
Application developers
build things.

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:44.520
And they get bundled
together, tested most times

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:45.930
by a separate team.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:49.230
There is a deployment team that
actually did the deployment.

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:52.200
There is a huge
orchestration around it,

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:53.700
because these
deployments are huge.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:55.440
And their costs
[INAUDIBLE],, right?

00:18:55.440 --> 00:18:58.560
So and that's disruptive
to the stores.

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:01.830
So you need to find out what's
a good time to deploy this.

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:06.270
And that require a whole team
of people to actually manage it.

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:10.660
How many other retailers
this sounds familiar?

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:12.750
So we were there.

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:20.550
So and then around 2015,
2016, 2016-ish time period--

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:24.190
of course, we started
embracing open source.

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:29.010
We have the Linux server
available in the store.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:32.040
And so people were able to
start building microservices.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:34.050
Great, right?

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:40.020
And then of course, our team
started using Chef cookbooks

00:19:40.020 --> 00:19:41.620
to start deploying, right?

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:42.120
It's great.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:43.740
And some other
challenges like Ethan

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:46.950
mentioned earlier related to
how people build cookbooks

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:50.310
and how it becomes complicated.

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:54.060
It started becoming
challenging for the teams.

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:58.810
They were able to deploy
it fairly successfully.

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:00.930
But it's more like 80%, right?

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:03.630
You still need to log into
a sufficient set of stores

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:04.470
to fix things.

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:06.450
It soon became
evident that teams

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:09.180
were spending like
sometimes up to 70%

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:14.970
of their developers' time
in actually fixing things

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:17.500
so that it will
work in the stores.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:19.903
So deployment was
like a nightmare.

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:28.120
So we needed two things
from this, right?

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:32.170
So we needed immutable images.

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:36.580
And we needed the ability
to safely deploy them

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:38.780
to the store.

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:42.490
And what I mean by
that is we don't want

00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:44.050
this mess about like--

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:46.300
when you talk about
1,800 stores we

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:51.955
need something that we are
guaranteed it will work.

00:20:51.955 --> 00:20:54.580
And containers were a
great concept, right?

00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:56.520
And it let developers do that.

00:20:56.520 --> 00:20:58.630
It also brought in
some other benefit,

00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:00.100
which is very profound.

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:02.800
We were suddenly
able to build things

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:06.760
once and be able to deploy in
the cloud for our e-commerce.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:08.230
And then we were
able to deploy it

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:11.560
in the stores for
our checkout lanes.

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:12.760
So that was profound.

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:15.820
So immutable images
and then we wanted

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:17.350
to deploy with no downtime.

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:19.300
One of the things
that I really wanted

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:23.620
the team to be able
to accomplish was we

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:25.970
wanted developers
to be able to safely

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:28.060
deploy during daytime even.

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:30.900
So what that means is
no downtime, right?

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:33.130
So Kubernetes let
us do all that.

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:34.030
So great.

00:21:34.030 --> 00:21:37.010
Then that's the reason
why we want Kubernetes.

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:39.670
So people agreed on that.

00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:43.450
Now with that said,
there are challenges.

00:21:43.450 --> 00:21:47.170
How are we going to implement
Kubernetes across 1,800 stores?

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:49.210
And how are you
going to manage it?

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:54.370
If it takes stores itself,
it's pretty small, right?

00:21:54.370 --> 00:21:58.580
So we don't have like a vast
Compute Cloud infrastructure

00:21:58.580 --> 00:21:59.080
there.

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:04.690
We have basically most of
the stores three blades

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:06.840
with a shared storage.

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:09.250
So it's not huge.

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:12.340
We have-- of course,
stores go offline.

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:13.570
So it can lose power.

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:15.070
It can lose network.

00:22:15.070 --> 00:22:18.340
So it need to be able to
operate by itself in some sort

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:21.070
of like an autonomous fashion.

00:22:21.070 --> 00:22:23.170
Then there are
other complications.

00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:24.560
How are we going to--

00:22:24.560 --> 00:22:27.400
if you do an
implementation, how are you

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:31.900
going to manage deployment
across 1,829 stores?

00:22:31.900 --> 00:22:34.720
How are we able to monitor it?

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:38.780
How are we able to budget
the resources appropriately?

00:22:38.780 --> 00:22:41.240
Those are all challenges.

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:43.570
So the team went
about solving it.

00:22:43.570 --> 00:22:48.130
And what they did was
they built what we call

00:22:48.130 --> 00:22:51.490
as a system called Unimatrix.

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:58.690
So Unimatrix acts as like
this centralized system

00:22:58.690 --> 00:23:03.410
for developers to interact
with this fleet of Kubernetes

00:23:03.410 --> 00:23:08.330
clusters, which
is 1,829 clusters.

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:12.730
And it will interact with
this fleet of 1,829 Kubernetes

00:23:12.730 --> 00:23:15.160
clusters in an asynchronous way.

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:22.690
And it implemented
Kubernetes API spec.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:24.820
And what that meant
for the developers

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:27.280
were that they can
interact with it

00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:32.830
as if they were interacting with
a big vast Kubernetes cluster.

00:23:32.830 --> 00:23:35.467
And at the same time--

00:23:35.467 --> 00:23:37.800
I don't know how many people
are [INAUDIBLE] on the John

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:41.030
Engelman's Spinnaker talk.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:43.540
We use Spinnaker for deployment.

00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:45.700
And Spinnaker natively
supports Kubernetes.

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:47.970
So we didn't have to
do anything there.

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:51.430
It just kind of
seamlessly fit right in.

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:55.390
Unimatrix does
primarily two things.

00:23:55.390 --> 00:24:01.800
It fetches the desired states
that gets applied to it.

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:04.190
And then it actually
distributes it off

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:08.630
to the fleet of
Kubernetes clusters.

00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:11.440
And then it, at
regular intervals,

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:14.300
fetches back the actual state.

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:16.890
So that worked really well.

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:19.820
We could let these
Kubernetes clusters work

00:24:19.820 --> 00:24:20.930
kind of autonomous.

00:24:20.930 --> 00:24:24.700
But for developers kind of
acted like a single Kubernetes

00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:26.510
cluster.

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:28.850
We solved the
problem of developers

00:24:28.850 --> 00:24:32.630
being able to build stuff and
then just kind of deploy it.

00:24:32.630 --> 00:24:34.250
And it's not as simple, right?

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:38.821
Like we don't build things and
just deploy it to 1,829 stores,

00:24:38.821 --> 00:24:39.320
right?

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:42.290
So there are nuances around it.

00:24:42.290 --> 00:24:44.270
So we need to build
more features, right?

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:47.980
So generally what
happens is developers

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:51.800
will build an
application or a feature

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:54.140
they want to deploy
to one store.

00:24:54.140 --> 00:24:56.570
In certain cases if
it's a proof of concept,

00:24:56.570 --> 00:24:59.990
they want to really send it
to like a subset of stores.

00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:02.990
Our stores itself are kind
of grouped into regions.

00:25:02.990 --> 00:25:04.940
And then we have different
format of stores--

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:07.760
small format, large format.

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:11.390
And we also have specialized
things in the stores,

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:16.670
like some have Starbucks, some
have liquor that they sell.

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:18.890
So we needed some
way to group it

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:21.590
so that people can
actually deploy them

00:25:21.590 --> 00:25:23.850
to this set of groups.

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:27.560
So we leverage Kubernetes
namespaces to do that.

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:29.250
And I don't know who came up--

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:30.980
which developer came
up with the concept

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.590
of using animal names-- but
they actually use animal names

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:34.670
to group stores.

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:37.002
So beetle is one store.

00:25:37.002 --> 00:25:40.690
It's exactly one store,
probably a store in Minneapolis,

00:25:40.690 --> 00:25:42.440
Coon Rapids store.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:47.340
And if it is
[INAUDIBLE] 299 stores.

00:25:47.340 --> 00:25:51.680
So and what in essence happens
is we store, of course,

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:54.440
these namespaces' desired
states at HQ, right,

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:57.290
at the central system.

00:25:57.290 --> 00:26:03.320
The beauty about this is
stores can go and of course,

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:04.400
work autonomously.

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:08.240
And if it goes down,
it can come back up

00:26:08.240 --> 00:26:10.770
and gets the desired state.

00:26:10.770 --> 00:26:13.597
And it will eventually
kind of get into that state

00:26:13.597 --> 00:26:14.180
it need to be.

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:21.990
The magical thing is if
you build a new store,

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:24.350
we just need to add
it to the group.

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:26.525
It will automatically
start working.

00:26:26.525 --> 00:26:30.440
It will identify which
applications it need to run.

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:31.680
It will download them.

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:32.580
It will run it.

00:26:32.580 --> 00:26:33.830
You don't need to do anything.

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:34.788
It's almost like magic.

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:42.410
So we solved that problem of
building groups and providing

00:26:42.410 --> 00:26:48.330
developers that ability to run
applications in these groups,

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:50.550
so to speak.

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:52.220
So that's where we are, right?

00:26:52.220 --> 00:26:57.620
So this is a real snapshot
of our production Spinnaker,

00:26:57.620 --> 00:27:01.340
where an application-- in this
case called promo engine--

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:03.710
being deployed to our stores.

00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:06.170
So they deployed it to
the group called tiger,

00:27:06.170 --> 00:27:08.300
which is a group of 600 stores.

00:27:08.300 --> 00:27:10.940
You can actually see that
they're running [INAUDIBLE]..

00:27:10.940 --> 00:27:14.630
And it is all green.

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:18.120
Promotion engine is something
that our cash registers use.

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.430
So it's pretty critical
for our stores.

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:27.840
Now so this is all working.

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:31.630
So where are we now?

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:36.660
So we started this
journey early last year.

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:41.100
And we onboarded our first
customers around peak season

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:42.780
last year.

00:27:42.780 --> 00:27:48.146
Now we are in all 1,829 stores.

00:27:48.146 --> 00:27:50.760
There are three
nodes in each store.

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:55.750
So 5,466 nodes are running.

00:27:55.750 --> 00:28:01.920
We have close to 200,000
pods running in production

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:03.840
across our chain.

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:07.350
And it's growing really, right?

00:28:07.350 --> 00:28:08.470
That's a state we are in.

00:28:11.920 --> 00:28:14.010
So the goals we set
out to accomplish--

00:28:14.010 --> 00:28:15.900
being able to deploy
immutable images,

00:28:15.900 --> 00:28:18.570
being able to safely deploy--

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:22.590
teams can now deploy safely
software to the stores

00:28:22.590 --> 00:28:25.020
during daytime when
the store is actually

00:28:25.020 --> 00:28:26.760
working in a safe fashion.

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:30.170
And they can do it
across our chain.

00:28:30.170 --> 00:28:33.270
That's story of
Kubernetes in stores.

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:34.170
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:35.070
Thank you.

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:35.670
Come on in.

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:39.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

